  "peirce_expected": {
    "firstness": "25 document files on a FAT32 volume, dated 2010–2011, each containing one or more email addresses. Formats span plain text, UTF-16, RTF, PDF, OLE2 binary (DOC/XLS), OOXML ZIP (DOCX/XLSX), nested containers (DOC-in-DOC, PPTX-in-DOCX, etc.), ZIP, ZIP-in-ZIP, GZIP, GZIP-in-GZIP, and iWork '09 (Pages/Keynote/Numbers).",
    "secondness": "The corpus is structurally self-consistent: every file uses its format's native mechanisms (RTF control words, PDF content streams, OLE2 streams, OOXML XML paths, DEFLATE compression). None of the encodings are anomalous relative to what the format specification allows. The nested containers (myfile22.zip, myfilegzip2.txt.gz.0.gz, docx_within_docx.docx) use standard ZIP and GZIP wrapping — unusual but not prohibited. Baseline deviation: the corpus appears purpose-built (Makefile present; all files share 2011-02-02 timestamp on a volume created specifically for tool testing).",
    "thirdness": "The corpus is a forensic tool validation dataset, not an operational covert channel. The Makefile confirms intentional construction for testing. The repeating pattern across 25 files is deliberate enumeration of encoding surfaces — analogous to a test suite, not a communication protocol. No evidence of evasion intent: the email addresses are plainly named (xls_cell@microsoft_excel.com, email_in_zip_zip@zipfile2.com) — an actual covert channel would not label itself."
  },
  "devil_advocate": "Every encoding technique found in this corpus has a legitimate, documented use: UTF-16 LE is mandated for Windows system files and many cross-platform document standards; RTF is a documented interchange format predating Unicode; PDF content streams are the standard mechanism for embedding rendered text; OLE2 compound documents are the original Microsoft Office format, still widely used; OOXML ZIP containers are the ISO/IEC 29500 standard for modern Office documents; embedded objects (DOCX-in-DOCX, PPT-in-DOC, XLS-in-DOC) are standard OLE object linking features used for document composition; ZIP and GZIP are ubiquitous compression standards; double-compression (ZIP-in-ZIP, GZIP-in-GZIP) occurs legitimately when compressed files are re-archived; iWork '09 formats are ZIP+XML — structurally identical to OOXML. The spreadsheet comment channel (workbook2.xlsx) is a documented Excel feature for cell annotations. None of these formats require legal justification. The corpus is a publicly available Digital Corpora research dataset (nps.edu/faculty/garfinkel/carpe/datasets/nps-2010-emails/) created specifically for validating forensic extraction tools. Expected verdict is NOISE: the encoding variety is the point of the corpus, not evidence of malicious intent.",
